    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d829">

                <pb facs="0804" n="706" sameAs="frus:frus1900/pg706"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Mr.
                            Powell.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1900-01-08"><hi rendition="#italic">January 8,
                        1900</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 390.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    No. 687, of the 16th ultimo, reporting that a question has arisen between the
                    Governments of Haiti and Germany because the courts of the Republic in certain
                    civil cases decline to take cognizance of them if both parties are foreigners
                    and either refuses his assent to the jurisdiction and competency of the
                    court.</p>
                <p>In reply, I have to say that article 6 of the treaty of 1864 between the United
                    States and Haiti, guarantees to American citizens the right which is in
                    controversy between Germany and Haiti. It is therefore unnecessary, for the
                    present at least, for the Department to express any opinion on the merits of the
                    controversy.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Hay.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
